# to the System V ones and left the old sources behind unbuilt: libc/omakefile,
# the older of the two, has `o/index.o: gen/index.c' at :140 and
# `o/rindex.o: gen/rindex.c' at :176 with both in its object list at :22 and
# :35, while libc/mkfile -- the one the build reads -- lists strchr.o (:57,
# :235) and strrchr.o (:59, :245) and neither index nor rindex.  gen/index.c
# and gen/rindex.c are still on the tape, complete and correct, in no archive:
# no member of libc.a or liboc.a defines _index, and gen/strchr.s declares only
# _strchr with no second entry point.
# THEY ARE THE SAME FUNCTION.  strchr.s's own header carries the C program it
# replaces -- "Return the ptr in sp at which the character c appears; NULL if
# not found" -- which is gen/index.c line for line, and calls it "the
# `standard' for the C-library".  Same arguments, same return, same result on
# not-found.
# ONE CALLER.  libpc/EXPO.c is the only file in cmd/pascal that names index at
# all, at :13 for the declaration and :18 for the call.  Rewriting it makes
# EXPO.c newer than the tape's prebuilt EXPO.o, so libpc's makefile:45-48
# recompiles that one object and re-archives -- on the guest `libpc' is the
# tape's ar file, which is why it read as up to date until now (the host
# checkout shows it unpacked into a directory of members, which is v10-tree.py's
# doing and not what mkv10 extracts).
# The two seds avoid matching 'e' -- a single quote inside a single-quoted sed
# expression ends the string, which is how the seventeen operator casts above
# silently did nothing on their first attempt.
cd $SRC/cmd/pascal/libpc || exit 1
sed \
    -e 's|extern char \*index();|extern char *strchr();|' \
    -e 's|cp = index(buf, |cp = strchr(buf, |' \
    EXPO.c > z$$ && mv z$$ EXPO.c

