# FIVE MORE alloc FAILURE TESTS, the same class as pmon.c above and the same
# reason the pass cannot see them: `if (cp == -1)' with cp a char * is EQ with
# one pointer and one integer, which trees.c:1815-1818 gives PUN, and
# chkpun exempts only an icon whose value is ZERO (trees.c:920-923).  NIL is
# 0.h:180's `#define NIL 0' so every `== NIL' in these files is exempt and only
# the -1 tests are not.  A modern compiler emits nothing for a pointer against
# -1 at any warning level, so these came from grepping every comparison
# against a non-zero literal in the units the makefile compiles and checking
# each name's declaration:
#	hash.c:149    register char *cp   (:118)
#	nl.c:600,604  char *cp
#	string.c:43   char *strngp        (:24, `STATIC char *strngp = strings')
#	tree.c:120    register char *cp
# The other seven hits are ints -- constcnt, typecnt, varcnt, yychar,
# OY.Yychar and getline() -- and yyget.c:243 is inside a comment.  The same
# grep over pi finds nothing but ints and a comment, which is corroborated by
# pi having compiled clean.
# Substituting on the expression rather than the whole line keeps this clear of
# the two indentations nl.c uses and of any quoting.
cd $SRC/cmd/pascal/pxp || exit 1
for f in hash.c nl.c tree.c
do
	sed 's|cp == -1|cp == (char *)-1|g' $f > z$$ && mv z$$ $f
done
sed 's|strngp == -1|strngp == (char *)-1|g' string.c > z$$ && mv z$$ string.c

# pmon.c:174 IS char * AGAINST long *, AND THE PASS WAS DROPPING IT ON PURPOSE.
# The note on the pi block above says int * against long * is not a combination
# ccom acts on, and the evidence for that is the machine: it compiled conv.c
# with `long *lp = dub;' and dub an int *.  That is true of THAT PAIR ONLY --
# SZLONG and SZINT are equal -- and the filter built from it threw away every
# diagnostic naming long * at all, including this one, which is a different
# pair: pmon.c:44 is `STATIC long *zbuf;', 0.h:220 is `char *alloc();', and
# chkpun compares type codes after DECREF, so CHAR against LONG fires where
# INT against LONG does not.  Narrowing the filter to the int*/long* pair alone
# turns up this line and nothing else in either pxp or pi.
#	cp = zbuf = alloc(i = (zcnt + 1) * sizeof *zbuf);
# is two assignments, char * = long * and long * = char *, so it takes the same
# shape as :177 three lines below it: the inner assignment parenthesised, each
# side cast to what it is declared.
cd $SRC/cmd/pascal/pxp || exit 1
sed \
    -e 's|^	cp = zbuf = alloc(i = (zcnt + 1) \* sizeof \*zbuf);$|	cp = (char *)(zbuf = (long *)alloc(i = (zcnt + 1) * sizeof *zbuf));|' \
    pmon.c > z$$ && mv z$$ pmon.c

# TWENTY-FIVE `clean' TARGETS ABANDON THEMSELVES ON AN UNBUILT TREE.  mk(1)
# 266-269 hands a recipe to `/bin/sh -e' and -e quits the whole recipe at the
# first command that returns non-zero, so `rm libdbm.a' in a directory where
# nothing has been built takes the rest of the recipe with it.  mkfile:29-39
# carries a `|| :' on all 220 of its `make clean' calls for exactly this, which
# works and hides the cause; these are the cause.
#
# THE COUNT IN THAT COMMENT IS WRONG IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, and this is what a scan
# of all 444 build files says instead:
#   - 26 clean targets run `rm' with no -f, not sixteen.  One of the 26,
#     cmd/f77/mkfile:64, already wraps its rm in `set +e ... set -e' and is
#     left alone, so 25 are repaired here.
#   - 111 build files have no clean target at all, not nine -- but the nine the
#     comment names include `ex', and cmd/ex/makefile:93-96 has one, written
#     `-rm -f' twice over.  Absence is already handled by the `|| :', so the 111
#     are recorded and not invented; what fails is the unguarded rm, not the
#     missing target.
#
# `rm -f' rather than make's `-' prefix, because seven of these are mkfiles and
# mk has no such prefix -- one form works in both dialects and this tree is
# converging on mk anyway.
cd $SRC/cmd/adb/11v || exit 1
sed 's|^	rm adb \$(FILES)$|	rm -f adb $(FILES)|' Makefile > c$$ && mv c$$ Makefile
cd $SRC/cmd/adb/68v || exit 1
